In <2sdrdd$7cj@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk> rwgm1@cus.cam.ac.uk (Rupert Moss-Eccardt) writes:

>I still maintain that if you blast huge rates of data at a PC serial port
>it won't be able to take it.  Mind you if you hide it in some protocol
>such as SLIP, PPP etc then it won't show.  Last time I did any timing tests
>(about 16 months ago) a VGA chip couldn't display anything close to 14400.
>Disk drives aren't much better.  I only mention this because I get upset with
>the tick-box approach to design that all manufacturers seem to go for these
>days.

I think you are a little out of date.  I log enough information
to be quite confident that the error levels are trivially small.
Running Worldblazers at 115.2 kbps, I get 2050 cps for compressed
news batches using turbo-PEP.  I get 4500 cps for very
compressible database transfers through PPP using
V.32bis/V.42/V.42bis.  I get 7800 cps for artificially
compressible data generated for transfer testing.  These figures
are completely consistent and reproducible.

I run a Metheus Premier video adaptor (ISA version) which will do
135000 X stones at 1024x768x8.  It will display 24 bits at
1024x768, 16 bits at 1280x1024 and 8 bits at 1600x1200.  It
definitely has no problem pumping a mere 1600cps of ascii text
out.  Newer S3 based boards are doing over 300K Xstones.  I have
an adaptec 1742 which will sustain 2.1MB/sec from a Quantum disk
and manages 3.0MB/sec throughput disk-to-disk.  Ok, it is nothing
near what I can get on, for example, the FWD controller on an HP
9000/735, but then the system cost 15% of the 735.

In any event, let me reiterate.  I'm not saying PC hardware is
wonderfully reliable, or a match for workstation performance in
general.  I'm saying that workstation vendors are hopelessly inept
when in comes to async devices and drivers, so much so that even
$30 PC hardware makes them look silly.  This argument that we
don't want CPUs wasting time on async processing is just plain
crazy.  I DO.  I think lots of people do.  I brought PCs into the
story just to show that the amount of CPU wasted on async
processing is trivial anyway.

I have a printer that can handle 57.6 kbps.  I can drive it at
that rate with my PC Unix system.  I've never seen an error.
Output only, so not surprising.  I can't drive it at that rate
with the aforementioned HP 735, and HP makes the printer.
