In article <matuscak-200594213550@zaphod.rohrer.com> matuscak@rohrer.com (Joe Matuscak) writes:

>
>Im using a TZ87 DLT tape as the backup device. While Ultrix 4.3A
>understands it, nsr doesnt, so I have to tell it the tape is a TZ86. 
>Apparently the OSF version does explicitly support the TZ87.  When I asked
>about that, the CSC folks kind of implied that the Ultrix version of nsr
>wasnt going to be enhanced any more. Care to comment?
>

Correct.  The ULTRIX version won't be enhanced anymore.  But calling
your TZ87 a TZ86 will work just fine.  The only thing you might want
to do is to specify a larger capacity when you label your tapes.
The TZ87 will hold 10GB in native mode, and about 20GB in compressed
mode (depending on how well your data compresses.)  Compare that to
6GB for a TZ86.  The only REAL difference the "capacity" makes is
when you display volume statistics.  I find it pretty annoying
to see that my volume is 125% full.  NetWorker will actually write
to the physical end of the tape in any case, so the label capacity is
purely advisory.

>One of the things id like to do is be able to have nsr keep the last weeks
>worth of daily backups, the weekly full backups for a month and the first
>full of a month for a year. It looks like nsr wants to keep everything
>around up to the retention times. Are there any plans to allow more
>elaborate history strategy?
>
I'm not aware of any plans to change NetWorker's history strategy for
AUTOMATIC index management.  Using the command line interface, it is
possible to delete individual save sets by SaveSet ID (SSID), but
it's pretty time consuming.  I'll do some more research on the
possibilities.
