                Supercomputing Symposium '94 Final Notice

  This is the final notice for advance registration for SS'94, Canada's
annual International High Performance Computing Conference and
Exhibition to be held in downtown Toronto on June 6-8. The advance
registration fee is only $250 (Canadian), a very small amount to pay
for the chance to attend the 65 information-packed sessions which have
been scheduled. Students can be observers for only $50.

  This summary is provided to allow prospective attendees to determine
whether SS'94 would be of interest to them. If you know anyone you believe
would be interested in SS'94, please forward this message to them.

  The five invited speakers are all world class experts in their fields,
and great speakers to boot. They include:
  + Norris Parker Smith [overview of high performance computing]
    * editor-at-large for the "HPCwire Select News" electronic magazine
    * coauthor of the book "The Supercomputer Era"
  + Horst Simon [description of experiences with MPP computing at NAS]
    * Computer Sciences Corporation department manager responsible for
      three groups of researchers in the areas of parallel algorithm
      development, scientific visualization, and grid generation. The
      researchers collaborate as contractor personnel with the staff of
      the NAS Applied Research Branch at NASA Ames.
  + John Simonds [discussion of digital recording for HDTV and the NII]
    * was Executive Director of the National Storage Industry Consortium
    * spent 39 years with Eastman Kodak's research organization
  + Andrew Bjerring [report on CANARIE]
    * President/CEO, Canadian Network for the Advancement of Research,
      Industry and Education]
    * senior Director of Information Technology (U. of Western Ontario)

  The other contributed papers cover a wide range of topics related to HPC:
software, hardware, programming, scientific and engineering applications,
and the Information Superhighway. In addition most of the major high
performance computer vendors will be represented with exhibits and talks.

  Because the number of papers (55 contributed and 10 vendor) is larger
than expected, sessions will be held during the afternoon of Wednesday,
June 8. These sessions will include many interesting presentations as well
as the panel discussion "Vendors' Views of the Future". If at all possible,
you should plan to stay at the conference through Wednesday afternoon.

  The conference social scene includes an early-bird reception, a gourmet
banquet/murder-mystery, and (optionally) a 20% discount on tickets to see
the Toronto production of Miss Saigon - to say nothing of the entire city
of Toronto.

  Additional details are available in the CONFERENCE INFORMATION AND
REGISTRATION MATERIALS document. If you do not already have a copy, you can
obtain one using one of the following methods:
  (1) give me your address and I will fax, mail or email it to you.
  (2) download it using anonymous FTP from node "ftp.utirc.utoronto.ca"
      in directory "/pub/SS94". The document is available in two formats:
      PostScript (registration.ps) and ASCII text (registration.txt).
The FTP directory also includes ASCII files describing the program:
"schedule.txt" contains the conference schedule of events and talks;
"papers.txt" contains a brief abstract of each talk.

