Malcolm Dunnett (dunnett@mala.bc.ca) wrote:
: In article <2r8r9k$fj1@ulowell.uml.edu>,
:   oneill@cs.uml.edu (Brian 'Doc' O'Neill) writes:
: > |>    Will it be possible to add these "future" CPUs into an existing 2100,
: > |> and have them coexist with the current CPU, or must all the CPUs in the
: > |> box be identical ( ie will I have to throw away the current CPU in order
: > |> to upgrade to follow-on versions )?
: > |> 
: > 
: > I doubt it. The 2100 is meant to be an SMP system. Can't do symmetrical
: > processing an assymetrical CPUs, really...
: > 

:   Perhaps I'm just being dense, but why not?

:   We need to define terms here. I'm not talking about some future architecture
: coexisting, I'm talking about a hypothetical near term follow on AXP board,
: using the same chip but at a faster clock rate and/or with a bigger cache ( eg
: 275MHZ chip, 4MB cache). 

So long as the boards are 'equivalent' at the bus interface level, this
is possible.  This requires more engineering effort.  One particularly
thorny problem is cache invalidation on other processors, a difficult
thing to handle on CPUs with different cache subsystems.

Assuming the hardware interlock/interaction is taken care of, SMP
systems should tolerate CPUs of varying speeds.  You may see some
undersirable characteristics when the discrepency becomes too large,
say an order of magnitude.  Problems can be caused by locks being held
longer than usual by processes with an affinity to slower CPUs. This
could cause other processes to block and result in higher response
times and lower total throughput.  Interrupt post-processing is also
an issue, but CPUs are already so much faster than most things causing
interrupts that this shouldn't be much trouble (except fddi/atm stuff).

That said, it would be reasonably easy to re-implement the same board
with a higher clock rate but otherwise exactly the same and have it
work.  I don't think DEC will do that.  I expect they will want to up
the speed and the cache size.  Like the VAX 6xxx line, they may change
the caching strategy requiring changes to memory boards as well as
processors.

It is almost certainty that if DEC announces an in-box upgrade for the
A500, you will need to change all CPUs in the box.

Glenn Hollinger,
University of Saskatchewan.


