I am using a DS700, a Mac and Versaterm SLIP right now.

Make sure that the internet address and subnet mask are set for the server.
If you expect to make connections off of the subnet specified by the address
and subnet mask, make sure that you specify a default gateway (router).

At this point, you should be able to make telnet connections from the terminal
server.  You should verify this.

The only characteristic that needs to be set on the port is to enable SLIP.
(In order to enable CSLIP, compression attributes must be configured.)

For the Mac, you need to set the IP address, subnet mask, Domain Name Server
address and Domain name.  This is done in the TCP control pannel.
After you do this, you will have to restart the Mac.
The address of the Mac must be within the subnet specified for the terminal
server.

Now you should be able to dial up the terminal server.  Use the Versaterm
AdminSlip Terminal Window to enter the command "CONNECT SLIP<cr>".  After
that select the "CONNECT" button in the Terminal Window and you should have
IP connectivity.

You can improve performance for some applications by enabling CSLIP on the
Mac and the terminal server.  The terminal server can be configured to
automatically detect CSLIP usage.

The scripting language in Versaterm SLIP can be used to automatically dial
into the terminal server, enter the password, enable/connect SLIP and bring
up the IP connection.  This is what I do from home.

Hugh

