In article <CqDsos.51LB@hawnews.watson.ibm.com> c1durko@watson.ibm.com (John Steven Durko) writes:
>What's the trick to getting the key marked (ESC) on the alpha keyboard to
>actually be ESCAPE.  I've found I can do ^[ to send an escape but this
>is rather kludgy.  What am I missing?


If you're in the OSF/X universe, you can munge with xmodmap and check
with xev.  This is what I do on startup with my (gak) SPARCstation 2 to
reset some keys;  it turns the BackSpace key into Delete, F9 into
Escape, and turns off a bunch of keys I kept landing on accidentally.

xmodmap -e "keysym BackSpace = Delete"
xmodmap -e "keysym F11 = NoSymbol"  -e "keysym F12 = NoSymbol"  -e "keysym F13 = NoSymbol"  -e "keysy\
m F14 = NoSymbol"  -e "keysym F15 = NoSymbol"
xmodmap -e "keysym F16 = NoSymbol"  -e "keysym F17 = NoSymbol"  -e "keysym F18 = NoSymbol"  -e "keysy\
m F19 = NoSymbol"  -e "keysym F20 = NoSymbol"
xmodmap -e "keysym F9 = Escape"
-- 
