In article <1994May26.172252.6597@vax.sbu.ac.uk>, hoskinw@vax.sbu.ac.uk writes:
>
>I have recently heard the two following comments and would appreciate other
>views on them:-
>
>* When taking a level 0 dump, the machine should first be placed in 
>  single user mode, and fsck should be run first (Ultrix or Unix system)

Dump reads the raw device and makes no attempt to coordinate the
backup of a file with other programs that might be writing it.
Further, when dump first starts it takes a snapshot of the file
system state and uses this through out the backup.  When a file
system is mounted it can change out from underneath dump and
dump won't see the changes.

The typical solution to ensure the file system is quiet when you
do the backup.  One way to ensure this is to do the backup with
the system running single user (and the file systems unmounted).

As for fsck, the only reason to run it is to fix any known file
system corruption or to check for file system corruption.  My
experience with most versions of ULTRIX is that there's little
reason to check, unless you think you have a hardware problem.
ULTRIX will want to insist on period file system checks after
the file system has been unmounted a certain number of times.
I think there is an option to tunefs(8) to set this value.

>
>* DAT tapes have a recommended use of only thirty uses before they are
>  replaced.

Never heard of such a thing.
