In article <2s3u68$fo9@uuneo.neosoft.com>, jleslie@dmccorp.com (Jerry Leslie) writes:
>Greg Kilcup (kilcup@pacific.mps.ohio-state.edu) wrote:
>
>: Did I read the specs concerning the serial port on the
>: 3000-300 series correctly?  According to the documents
>: served by www.digital.com it runs at only 19.2Kbits/sec,
>: about a factor 8 slower than what I expect out of my
>: next workstation purchase.
>
Gregory;

  The DEC 3000/300 series will run the serial port at up to 38400 bps.
The baud rate definitions are in /usr/sys/include/sys/termios.h (OSF/1).
Our company philosophy (if it can be called that :)) is that higher
rates than 38400 should be moved to a terminal server where they won't
slow down the cpu by interrupting at a high character-by-character
rate. If you're planning on running SLIP/PPP and want the higher rate,
you can try a terminal server such as the 90M or 90TL. If you run
CSLIP or transfer pre-compressed files via ftp, etc., then you generally
don't want to run v.42bis/MNP compression anyway, as this will actually
slow downt the transfer! Better bet is to get a v.fast 28800 modem,
and run the port at 38400 and an uncompressed carrier rate of 28800.

