In article <1994May26.220411.1252@nntpd2.cxo.dec.com>, alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.) writes:
>>
>>* DAT tapes have a recommended use of only thirty uses before they are
>>  replaced.
>
>Never heard of such a thing.

  Most helical-scan tape technology imposes limitations on media and
head life due to the relatively high wear of the spinning drum and
relatively long tape path. Videotape systems, which have a much
higher wrap-angle than DAT, and therefore a longer tape path, can be
expected to wear out a tape in about 200-300 passes. DAT tapes use a
shorter "M" wrap, but the actual life of the tape is determined by
quite a long list of factors, such as humidity, head condition,
guide condition, and the quality of the tape.... Helical scan tape
heads are good for about 3000 hours. Similar things can be said about
8mm tape. There ARE reasons that we still have DLT products in our
catalogs. Admittedly, DAT and 8mm do a great job at a pretty low price,
but like a new set of tires, they will eventually wear out!

Craig (who spent 15 years as a Broadcast TV Videotape engineer
