This pitiful "philosophy" is an outstanding example of why DEC 
appears to be going down for the count.

DEC workstation users have been clamoring for YEARS for quality serial 
ports (full modem control + hw flow + at least 57.6Kbps) instead of
what DEC gave them (how about the pathetic ports on the 3100 - WORSE
than what the lowly VS2000s came with!)  But DEC, in its superior
technical wisdom, KNOWS what's good for you.  It's not GOOD for you
to waste your "valuable" workstation cycles on something so degrading
as processing character I/O.  Instead, you should buy a dedicated
terminal server to handle the job.

OK, children friends at DEC: why don't you take a deep breath and THINK
about how people might use your products in the real world (assuming
you could sell any.)  For example, I might want to have an 3000/300
or DECpc 150 AT HOME (what a concept!)  And I might want to NETWORK
that box with the rest of the world (startling, eh?)  And maybe I
don't have dedicated singlemode fiber coming into my house, but only
an analog phone line.  No ISDN, no Ethernet-over-CATV, just POTS ...
can ya dig it?

So, given that scenario, I want to squeeze all the juice out of that
phone line for as little money as possible.  Fortunately, I can RIGHT
NOW buy a V.FC 28.8Kbps modem for $300, that would LOVE to talk 
to a 115.2Kbps serial port.  And this modem might actually be expected
to deliver about 60Kbps of real-world throughput (for typical NFS/FTP/
TELNET/SMTP/X11 data) IF I could find a serial port that fast.

Oh, but DEC, in their superior wisdom, knows that I don't want to BURDEN
my poor little 175MHz CPU with the grueling task of moving characters
at 14Kcps!  Instead it would be BETTER for me to buy a standalone 
terminal server for $2000 or whatever JUST to talk to this one modem!

How about this way of looking at things, DEC: if I'm a telecommuter,
then the speed of the serial port on the workstation is a very
significant determinant of the quality of my life.  So if I need to
buy a $2000 terminal server alongside a DEC workstation so that it
can keep up with a competing workstation that has 115.2Kbps built in,
then that's how much more the DEC box costs to me.  So guess what:
you lose!

Really, the lameness of DEC's stance on this issue is practically 
cosmic (comic?)  "[H]igher rates than 38400 [...] will slow down the
CPU by interrupting at a high character-by-character rate" indeed!
I happen to know for a fact that a 2.4 MIP VAX-3400 is *perfectly*
capable of handling a 38.4Kbps SLIP line [on a CXY08] at full 
throttle with plenty of CPU cycles to burn.  And you have the gall
to sit there and tell me that a damn 175Mhz, 84SPECint, blah blah 
