Just a rough idea, but you get the drift.  If I could buy one of these
today the check would already be in the mail.  Sure, the margins are razor
thin, but nobody sells boxes in this range with a big margin.  If Digital
knew what they were doing they could probably squeeze out 10% on the 1000
and a bit more on the 1100 (because the options are more high powered and
so would cost more).  Except for the processor and some of the options,
these are strictly commodity machines and so should be cheap to build.  Some
of the drivers may not exist yet, but certainly if dozens of small
companies can crank out SVGA drivers for Windows Digital can do so for VMS!
Deck out a 1000-100 with a 17" monitor, keyboard, mouse, and CD-ROM and
bring it home for around $2800, complete.  The prices are roughly in line
with Mac prices, except that these have more horsepower.  If Apple can do
it, why not Digital?  Note also that these come with some application
software, which would be necessary since you can't step into Egghead and
purchase Microsoft Word for VMS.

These aren't named "Chameleon" for nothing: there would also be an OSF/1
version, with equivalent software.  As for WNT, the OS would be there, but
users would have to buy their own software. 

Now some might argue that Digital cannot afford to sell anything this
inexpensive, to which I reply, they cannot afford not to.  A couple of
million of these in the field would produce steady revenues from software
upgrades, hardware upgrades, sales of manuals (note that only electronic
manuals are supplied), service contracts, repairs, etc.  There is
already software for these machines, although the manufacturers of it would
have to adjust their prices down to something reasonable if they expect to 
sell any. While it appears that Digital is "giving away" DECwrite, Motif
and the rest of the software, what they would really be doing is getting
their foot into a mass market that they would have some control over.   I
would expect that at these prices Digital would not allow CSLG/TEI prices
for educational sites, that the single user license would be enforced, and
that there would be little if any discounting.  Fair enough. 

Is there a market for these boxes?  The only way to tell is to put them on 
the market and see what happens.

