>In article <2r092g$6tv@coranto.ucs.mun.ca> frederic@morgan.ucs.mun.ca (Frederick Todd Janes) writes:
>>From: frederic@morgan.ucs.mun.ca (Frederick Todd Janes)
>>Subject: HELP! connecting HS modems to DECserver 200 MC
>>Date: 13 May 1994 16:15:12 GMT
>
>>I am trying to add 3 dial-in 14,4 modems to a DECserver 200MC.  
>
>>        2 hayes smartmodem optima 144 modems and
>>        1 USR sportster 14,400 fax modem.
>
>>the server port settings are:
>>        flow control XON, modem control ENABLED, N 8, access LOCAL, 
>>        type SOFT, and the following enabled characteristics:
>
>>autobaud, autoconnect, autoprompt, broadcast, dialup, DTRwait, 
>>inactivity timeout, input flow control, loss notification, message
>>codes, output flow control, and verification.
>
>OK, I haven't had to deal with your *exact* situation before, but I
>do see a couple of things that might help.
>
>1)  It sounds like you're only using Software handshaking on the
>Server's DTE.  Although this should have nothing to do with
>whether or not the modems actually can talk to each other,
>if you use Hardware handshaking here, you will have much
>better performance once you figure out the modem configurations.
>
>2)  If the meaning of "autobaud" here is the same as I'm thinking of
>it, then it means that the Server will attempt to adjust its DTE port
>speed to that of the incoming caller's baud.  This is bad!  It's
>usually easier and more reliable to set the DTE speed to the 
>highest speed that both the modem and the Server support.
>Hopefully, this is 57,600 baud.  If this won't work (I know that some
>old Sun machines aren't good at even 38,400), try 38,400 or even
>19,200, as a last resort.  Lock the port at this speed!  Then, the
>modem will adjust to the incoming caller's speed, and the
>port speed will remain the same.  This usually works best, or
>at least it did for me on a Sun SPARCserver.
>

The DECserver 200MC is an ancient beast with a maximum port speed of
19200 bps. Configure the ports for RTS/CTS flow control, no X-On/X-Off,
and hard-set the baud rate to 19200. Hayes-compatible v.32bis modems
can be attached and will run ok as long as you run a 14400 carrier
rate WITHOUT data compression. If you're going to be running CSLIP
over these lines and/or transferring compressed files, then compression
won't help anyway. You will want to initialize the Hayes-compatible
modems with 'AT&Q5S37=11N0%C0&K3&S1^M', where:

&Q5 - Negotiate an error-correcting link if possible

S37=11 - Use 14400 carrier (see N0)

N0 - Do not negotiate speed, use carrier speed setting from register S37.

%C0 - Do not use data compression

&K3 - Use RTS/CTS flow control

&S1 - Turn on DSR after handshaking

When the above conditions are met, Hayes-compatible modems will communicate
with the CPU at 19200 baud, which should match the setting of the 200MC.
If you enable compression (%C1), then the modem will attempt to communicate
with the CPU at the maximum rate for the specified carrier, i.e., 38400
for 9600 carrier, and 57600 for 14400 carrier; neither of which are
supported by the 200MC.

Newer DEC terminal servers support higher rates, generally to 115 KBps.
Some, such as the 90M, 90TL, 900TM, and 700 series also support SLIP/PPP
directly in the terminal server by use of the Network Access Software
package.

