--

On the other hand, I think this is one of the smartest things Digital
could be doing right now.

The truely smartest is the move to PCI-based machines.

I have three 3000-400's - they come with a (very good) private bus
(turbochannel) and their own form-factor. However, to do all of this,
you need to pay a bunch of people to design the power system, cabling,
box design, model airflow, manufactor the skin, etc etc.

There's precious little margin in all of this stuff, and a lot of
overhead. If Jane Public can buy a $1200 486-50DX2 PC that does what
they want, why they hell should they pay all this extra overhead for a
workstation?

By moving to commodity parts and interfaces, Digital will open their
products to other peoples periph's and make their periphs available
for others. It's no secret that Digitl is working on a host of
PCI-based chips (ethernets, bridges, graphics, etc). They'll have a
broader market.

Likewise, the PC-world has left the UNIX world and the VMS world in
the dust when it comes to *software*, which is why people buy
computers.


I think that Digital could do very well by providing an Windows-NT or
Windows emulation for OSF/1 and OpenVMS. This lets people use OSF/1
for what it does well (networking, file serving, etc) but still use
the less expensive Windows-NT software.



