$ 0.02 Solution to Make DEC Viable Again:
-----------------------------------------

1. Allow customers to give performance appraisels to Direct Sales. Those 
   who haven't been assigned new customers yet would be reviewed by the 
   last customers they were assigned to.

   There are some good folks in Direct Sales, but from personal experience,
   and comments from others, a large percentage are nearly worthless.

   The money saved here would be spent on the ESTOREs, making the 
   menu systems request experience level, so anyone could get a quote
   that covered, without having to call anyone :

   o trade-ins of older DEC, or non-DEC gear
   o software costs for licenses, support, documentation, in non-jargon.
   o maintenance cost after warranty expiration.
   o any special programs (Attention DEC shoppers, there's a flashing..")

2. Restart the R&D effort to make VMS machine-independent, based on 
   whatever kernel is in vogure: Mach, Taligent, Linux. Add a GUI
   interface at leas as good as HP-UX. Include pipes, which is one of
   the few good feature that Unix has that's missing from VMS.

3. Migrate all programming languages, including BLISS, DIBOL, to the
   new VMS.

4. Bundle in DFO, Notes, a good TCP/IP, and DECnet Phase IV.

5. Make a Internet gateway/router software package that DEC would donate
   free, to drive the less-secure Unix systems off the net. Not all Unix
   systems, just the ones with the most noticed in 'cert_advisories'. 
   This package could help dry up the used equipment market, as old 
   VAXStations replaced Intel-based systems running some sect of Unix.

6. Price the software in the same vein as Borland: cheaper to buy that
   to steal.

7. Make a version of the AXP chip for some mass market: video games,
   home energy management*, automobile systems controls, etc, so that 
   the per-unit cost is less than the PowerPC or Intel's Pentium.

8. Offer lap-top versions of VAXes and ALPHAs (apparently some were shown
   at DECUS ?)

9. Be prepared to split hardware design and production from software
   development.

10. Drop products like: modems, proprietary technology disks (this looks
    like it's happening), retire DLT tape drives, but offer DLT users a 
    trade-in on 4mm DAT drives.

--Jerry Leslie, definitely speaking for only myself.

* the nation's electric utilities are willing to spend ~$900/per home to
  offer remote & automatic power management modules, plus fiber, to avoid
  having to build new plants, per an article in the June, 1994 issue of
  "LAN Magazine". The main obstacle is the Public Utilities Holding Act,
  that prevents interstate utilities from investing in non-power-related 
  ventures. 

  IMHO, the utilities provide the second-most reliable service, next to
  natural gas companies (but they don't service all homes like the
  electric utilities do). The phone companies have had their chance at
  the "Info Super Highway" with ISDN, and some have made it affordable.
  And the cable TV companies service is improving, but would you want your
  job to depend on their service to your home ?

