Once upon a time, I posted a message to comp.os.vms about
a "mystery connector" on a MicroVAX 3100 model 10 I had 
assumed responsibility over.

> On the back, there is an Amphenol-type connector that goes to a
> block of 8 MMJ connectors.  Golly; these must be serial ports
> TXA0-TXA7.  There are your basic SCSI, net, console, and printer
> connections.  There is also, however, a female DB50 connector.
> I have no clue what that is--any ideas?
> 
> Also, everytime the system does an AUTOCONFIGURE it generates 
> this error message:
> 
> SYSGEN>  A A/LOG
>  PKA0
>  PKB0
>  ESA0
>  TXA0
> %SYSGEN-W-OPENIN, error opening SYS$COMMON:[SYSEXE]ZSDRIVER.EXE; as
> input
> %SYSGEN-E-FNF, file not found
>  ZSA0
> 
>I have never encountered a ZS device.  Could this be the mysterious
>DB50 connector?  If anyone can offer guidance, I would certainly
>appreciate it.

I received a few responses; the concensus was that my 3100
had a synchronous communication interface installed.  One 
person said I could simply remove the daughter card and that
would eliminate the message/problem.

Well, I finally got around to opening the system up to take a
gander at what I had.  Now I am even more confused.  

There was, of course, the mother board.  Towards the front,
there was a 4 Mb (wow!) memory daughter board plugged into the
mother board.

Here is where I get confused.  The was another daughter board
plugged into momma.  It had two different 50-class part numbers:
50-18904-01 and 54-18905-01 and claimed to be a "Combo Controller."
This board had two 40(?)-pin ribbon connectors, only one of which
was used.  A ribbon cable connected this combo controller board to
yet another board.  This additional board had ribbon cable connections
to the combo controller and the mother board.  The 37-pin Amphenol
and mysterious DB50 connectors were mounted on this board.  This 
board also had connectors where the two SCSI busses originated from.
This board also had two different 50-class part numbers printed on
it:  50-18857-01 and 54-18858-01.  The only other idenfication was
the letters LPWR.  The four connectors on the board were called
TH1, TH2, TH3, and TH4.

So, do I really have a synchronous option in this system?  Can anyone
give me a better idea what the different ancillary boards inside
actually are?  Thank you for the help!
