nsr (aka Networker) uses its own format. In fact, when used properly
various backups are intermingled on the tape. This means that you
can only read the tapes with nsr/networker. 

While this could be considered a drawback, my experience suggests that
the advantages this gives you outweigh this. 

The server keeps itself backed up and it is easy to restore a trashed
server (its worth doing the test to convince yourself ;) ). My policy
is to have a backup of the server, with the nsr software, done
using normal UNIX backup so that the basic server can be restored
quickly. The nsr backup can then be used to get the server uptodate.
If you don't do this, you have to do a reinstall of the basic os and
nsr.

My experience, for what it's worth, is that Networker does work and it's
easy to use. Our ops certainly like it.

Cheers,
