In article <Cq7tKF.6pJ@acsu.buffalo.edu>,
Chris Kalisiak <kalisiak@cs.buffalo.edu> wrote:
>>It's probably not worth a whole lot now, but they sure are robust.

>Unless the stepper motor that controls the horizontal motion dies. 
>At that point it will carriage return too far and crash into the 
>stopping screw to the left of the head. Everything stops and the 
>'paper out' light flashes and the beeper sounds.

Actually it's not a stepper motor but a PM motor, that's why it's so
fast.  Instead of commanding the head motor to step and hoping for the
best (like the LA30 does, which is why they tend to print entire characters
as one vertical line if the timing is just a little off), the LA120 starts
the motor going and uses a little aperture wheel to see when the head passes
the correct position (at full speed), at which point it fires the pins.

This sounds more like one of the big power transistors that drives the
PM motor from the +/- 24V supplies freaked out and shorted.  I've tried
temporarily jumpering the motor straight to the supply to test it, you're
right it makes quite a WHACK.

John Wilson


