Is this the right forum to discuss DEC's imminent demise?
The Dear Mister Robert Palmer and his band appear to be
ill-equipped to do anything but chop masts and tear down
sails.  The bilge is already flooded, and all Dear Mister
Robert Palmer and crew can do is pour concrete to plug
the leaks, and the poor ship inches a little bit further
to the bottom of the ocean floor.

This is compounded by seven years of product misplanning,
bad ergonomics, terrible GUIs, and an institutional frame
of mind not unlike that of governments that used to hide
behind an iron curtain or some such thing.  Someone must
have broken a mirror at DEC, but nobody there seems to
show any interest in describing these seven years of bad
luck.

After totally blowing a potentially lucrative alliance
with Apple, DEC lost its one major outlet for Alpha chips.
To top that, all this Alpha hoopla seems to have launched
DEC squarely into the middle of a big financial ocean, with
no land in sight.  It boggles, I say, to watch DEC blow
an unheard of lead in the microprocessor market.  It boggles,
I say again, to watch DEC committing precious resources to
running Windows NT, but nobody ever accused MicroBarge of
harboring any visionaries, either.

A friend and former DEC employee says:

> I'd really like to tell you you're wrong, but you're not.
> I think things are FUBAR at DEC.  Way too much chaos.

The Dear Mister Robert Palmer does not understand that DEC
hath not "two years" to turn itself around.  In this
industry, two years are equal to half a million geologic
years.  Pity the fool on a sinking ship, the crew emaciated
and diseased, climbing on a short mast to proclaim "Land 
Ahoy, Only 63,072,000,000 Milliseconds To Go!!!", pity the
fool indeed.

Now we have a boneheaded advertising campaign.  Cry glory
to the fatherland, I suppose, but it's all a bit pyrrhic,
and nobody's won anything to boat--um, I mean, to boot.
This is not going to get turned around like Chrysler or GM;
in the car business, people will wait out a few years of
incompetent management, but a VAX doth not an automobile
make.  With the currents too swift, the eddies too vicious,
much of the talent that can rescue DEC has long since
departed for calmer waters.

DEC will announce its own liquidation by March 1995, at the
latest.  Would anyone out there care to wager a flame or two?
Anyone from DEC who's willing to speak candidly?


