The DEMPR/DELNI combination is perfectly manageable so long as you
understand the basic limitations of that configuration.

The problem arises in that the DELNI (not a repeater, so it doesn't
count in the 5-4-3 rule) is a multiport transceiver that broadcasts
to all eight local ports. If the Global (9th) port of the DELNI is
connected to a transceiver with SQE Test (aka Heartbeat) enabled, then
the heartbeat will be sent out to anything connected to any of the
local DELNI ports. If one of these ports has a DEMPR on in, the DEMPR
(like all good 802.3-compliant repeaters) will interpret the heartbeat
as a collision fragment and jam all of it's ports for 9.6 microseconds
minimum.

Disabling heartbeat on the top-level transceiver (the one that the
DELNI is plugged into) eliminates the problem. So (as an earlier reply
suggests) does placing the DEMPR directly on the backbone rather than
through a DELNI.

	/Harry Sutton
	 Complex Problem Manager (Networks)
	 Multivendor Systems Engineering


