> |> to upgrade to follow-on versions )?
> |> 
> 
> I doubt it. The 2100 is meant to be an SMP system. Can't do symmetrical
> processing an assymetrical CPUs, really...
> 

  Perhaps I'm just being dense, but why not?

  We need to define terms here. I'm not talking about some future architecture
coexisting, I'm talking about a hypothetical near term follow on AXP board,
using the same chip but at a faster clock rate and/or with a bigger cache ( eg
275MHZ chip, 4MB cache). 

  I presume that these 2 boards would be indistinguishable to any software, so
why couldn't they coexist in an SMP system? SMP doesn't mean "CPUs operating
in lockstep" - it means each CPU is capable of picking up any instruction
thread at any point and executing it. The speed at which said CPU executes
the instructions wouldn't seem to be relevant.

  I'm only moderately familiar with the internals of VMS SMP on VAX, and don't
know how it differs on AXP, but I don't see any technical reason different
speed CPUs would preclude SMP operation.

