In article <CpJ4BJ.5zy@walter.bellcore.com>, kenwm@nyquist..bellcore.com (Kenneth Moyers 21123) writes:
> The bearings for the RD53 in my MicroVaxII just zinged.

  Are you sure it's the bearings and not the spindle ground strap? Take the
drive out of the cabinet and look at the logic board on the bottom of the
drive. There are 2 screws on one end and 2 hinges on the other. Carefully
loosen the 2 screws and swing the logic out on the hinges. You'll see a cop-
per strap with a graphite button on it in the middle of the logic card. If
you believe in spindle grounds on small drives, just bend it a little so it
hits the spindle in a slightly different spot. Otherwise, just unscrew the
whole spring assembly and discard it.

> I have a spare
> RD54 taken from an external drive box, but the Mvax dosn't seem to
> recognize it.  Can someone tell me what the jumper settings on the RD54
> should be?  Any other advice on getting the RD54 going in place of the 
> RD53 would be appreciated.

  First, you need an RQDX3 (M7555) controller. If you have an RQDX2 (or an
RQDX1 w/ RQDX2 ROMs) you can't use an RD54. If your RQDX3 is older, you may
also need an FCO to support RD54's properly.

  Next, make sure the drive is set to the proper select. The first hard disk
drive in a DEC system wants to be set to the 3rd drive select (either #2 when
the numbering sequence is 0-1-2-3 or #3 when it's 1-2-3-4). The Maxtors that
DEC used usually have jumpers labeled 1-C-2 3-C-4, so you'll want 3-C jumper-
ed and the others open. There are some variants that go 1-2-3-4-5-6, so you
would want 4-5 on those.

  Last, the drive needs to have already been formatted on either an RQDX3 or
a MicroVAX/VAXstation 2000. The "customer formatter" won't format non-DEC
drives on VAXes.

