chandoni@husc.harvard.edu writes:

>I wrote:
>>Is there a working version of GNU C++ for the alpha?  According to the
>>readme in 2.5.8, gcc only works on C, for now (until 2.6).

>Actually, 2.5.8 seems to work fine as a C++ compiler; it's libg++ that
>isn't yet supported.  My own classes work fine, as long as they
>use stdio instead of iostream (and other library functions).

>If anybody has gotten libg++ to work, let me know!

Both g++ and libg++ are functional on alphas.  There is a major
bug in each that can be patched fairly easily.

gcc-2.5.8 - In one of the cp- files there is a comment about 'this
	breaking vtables on the alpha'.  Grep for it and change the
	use of integer_type -> ptr_diff_type or something of the appropriate
	size.

libg++-2.5.3 - floatconv.c will core when handling floats.  Search for
	the definitions of the macros word0, word1.  Change the use of
	long -> int.  This seems to ameliorate things.

The remaining alpha specific bugs are avoidable and are beyond my power
to rectify.

	- Don't bother trying to optimize.  Missing symbols will result

	- Always link staticly or you get complaints at run time
	that abort has relocation information unexpectedly.

	- Bad code for
	  String * tmp = new bob[size];
	  for(size_t i = 4 ; ....

	  i and tmp co-exist

	- internal compiler error for dynamic arrays of objects with ctors

	- allocation of zero sized arrays causes corage

Good Luck
-- 

