I own a 5000/25.  Originally it had 12M RAM and a 426M disk, both of which
were woefully inadequate (I can't imagine squeezing a reasonable configura-
tion onto a 250M disk).

Upgrading the memory to 24M resulted in a *tremendous* performance increase.  
(I've since put another 16M in it for a total of 40M)  While the DECwindows/
Motif stuff is nice, the MIT X11R5 distribution is a hell of a lot smaller 
and faster; it installs relatively easily (I haven't had an opportunity to 
try out X11R6, yet).  The disk shortage was easily rectified by putting on 
a Micropolis 2210 1.05G drive, and a Bernoulli Transportable 90; both of 
these plug right up to the built-in SCSI port.

With a 24M or more of RAM and a decent sized disk, the machine's performance
is remarkably good.  You can get complete performance specs from Digital's 
WWW server; URL is 
   http://www.digital.com/pub/Digital/info/html/performance/sys/
     decsta-family-r3000.abs.html

The document with the specs has a table on page 10 that compares various 
benchmarks for all of the 5000 series (R3000 chip) DECstations, with
SPARC, HP, and SGI workstations.  The 5000/25 meets or exceeds the SPARC 
IPC and ELC on SPECmarks, SPEC int/float, MIPS, Dhrystone, etc.

I think it's a great machine.  I like the 5000/240 I do SysAdmin on, too.
