Does anyone have any performance information on the new mutiprocessor
DEC 2100 Model 500MP?

I'm especially interested in its performance as a function of number
of CPU's?

I was reading the April 18th edition of Digital News and Review and
was somewhat disturbed by what I saw.

Specifically it was comparing a four processor 2100 to a 2 processor
4000 (both systems used 190 MHz alpha, but the 4000 had significantly
more cache per processor, 4Meg vs 1Meg per processor).

The disturbing result to me is that the four processor 2100 had a PPI
(that's the benchmark metric they used (100 PPI = 60 MHz Dec pentium
machine) of about 700, a 1 processor 2100 had a PPI of 350, a 1 processor 
4000 had a PPI of 400, and a 2 processor 4000 had PPI of 600.

This tells me that the 4000 had a 600/400 = 1.5 increase in the
benchmark metric when going from 1 to 2 processors. This isn't great
in my opinion, but maybe acceptable.

The 2100 on the other hand had a 700/350 = 2.0 increase in the 
benchmark metric when going from 1 to 4 processors. That in my opinion
is horrible. Four times the processors only gives 2 times the throughput?

I don't know what the exact benchmark involved other than multiple 
copies of the same program, but a 2-fold increase in throughput for
a 4-fold increase in number of processors is pitiful.

Is this a particular benchmark that is designed not to scale well?
The reason I ask is that both the 4000 and the 2100 seem to scale
well on published Specrate_int and Specrate_fp: going from 1 to 2
processors in either case gives an increase of about 1.9 in both cases.



In comparison, we have a 4 processor SGI Onyx ( 4 100 MHz Mips R4400)
that on every real program I've thrown at it, if I throw multiple copies
of the same program (regardless of if it is cpu or memory intensive; I have
in-house developed applications that I'm using here, some of which are cpu
(both integer and fp) intensive and some of which are memory intensive)
I get at least a 3.9 times increase in throughput as compared to running
on just one of its processors. In other words, the SGI has almost
linear scaling of throughput verses number of processors.

Any insights into this? 

Does anyone have one of the 2100's with multiple processors? Does it
have better scaling than the above indicates?

By the way, our in-house applications run very well on the single cpu
alpha workstations such as the 3000/800 (at least twice as fast as
a single SGI processor). I am hoping that a 2 processor 2100 would match
our 4 processor Onyx in cpu computing power, but now I'm not so sure.
So far, trying to convince DEC to loan us a 2100 to test our software
on hasn't gotten us very far. If I could convince myself that it will
meet our needs we are ready to buy one, so this isn't frivolous
speculation on our part.

Also, an unrelated question: what is the justification for the OSF version
of the 2100 being $8000 more expensive than an equivalently equiped NT
version of the machine? It's my understanding that they use the exact same
hardware, the only difference is the OS.




-- 

   Tim Cullip
