Nikos Massios (nxm@dcs.ed.ac.uk) wrote:
: Anybody knows what mircocode control is?

Hmmmm.  Well since no one else is biting ...

Most (all?) processors use microcode to control their hardwire these 
days.  I believe in ye olde days (Z80, etc) the operation of the CPU was 
defined by the location of gates alone, but now processor designers 
use a mixture of hardware and software (microcode).

So machine code is what your VAX macro is turned into, but the execution of
this is controlled by microcode (hence "microcode control").  I'd guess 
that it would be used more on CISC machines with pipelines, e.g. the 
VAX 8800 has absolute scads of microcode.

Anyway, I've made a big enough fool of myself, someone who's actually taken
microprocessor design 101 can explain this better...

Hope that this helps.  BTW, I don'y normally follow this newsgroup.
