Peter da Silva (peter@nmti.com) wrote:
: >In article <2qdstc$f0a@umd5.umd.edu>,  <bleau@umdsp.umd.edu> wrote:
: >> 2) I thought 4mm DAT was a standard

: >There are at least two standards (DATA/DAT and I think DDS) and all sorts of
: >variations of compression in each type. It's like having a 5 1/4" floppy in
: >the late '70s, when everyone did their own formats.

DATA/DAT is dead, although there are still such drives out there. I
doubt any are still sold other than to those requiring duplication of
existing drives. 

The new Kid On The Block is DDS-2, which drives use 120 meter tapes,
and write tracks more densely. Vendors often quote an uncompressed
capacity and an 'up to' capacity after compression, typically an
unrealistic 4X. 

         60 meter   90 meter    120 meter
   DDS    1.3 GB      2.0 GB       n/a      (uncompressed)
  DDS-2   1.3 GB      2.0 GB     4.0 GB     (uncompressed)

Note that DDS-2 drives, when loaded with a 60- or 90-meter tape, will
write DDS-format tapes (but at the typically higher DDS-2 drive
speeds).  Thus, the DDS-2 drives are read- and write-compatible with
DDS drives if you use the older tapes. 

We've been using the HP 1533A drive, a DDS-2 drive with 512 KB/sec
transfer rate, for about a month now. We're very happy with it and are
getting more. 

