In article <2qgvm9$12l@inews.intel.com>, 
    			brodesky@td2cad.intel.com (Michael Brodesky) writes:
 
> Let say you have two systems. And you would like to create
> a directory on system 1 from a comfile on system 2.
> 
> system_2?  create /dir system_1::disk:[new_directory]
> 
> But the command fails due to invalid file specification. Is it possible
> to do this???

        It's possible, but not in the direct way you're trying to do it.
    DECnet makes many file-related  operations  transparent, but not all
    file-related    commands   support   remote   file   specifications.
    CREATE/DIRECTORY is in the latter category.

        It's been a long time since I've done this, so I'm only going to
    sketch the method.   You  use  a  DECnet  _task_  to execute the DCL
    command  on the remote node.  That task is just a command file which
    resides on the target machine, presumably in your  login  directory.
    The  command  file,  when  activated, opens SYS$NET READ/WRITE.  You
    have it read the DCL  command  from  SYS$NET, execute it, then close
    SYS$NET to let your end know that the task is complete.

        The version I had for doing  this  sort of thing used a "master"
    command  file  on the local node, and a "slave" on the remote  node.
    With a little effort, I could dig these up.  I haven't used them  in
    ages since there is little need for them in a cluster.  :-)

        -Ken
