In article <JS9S.94May8124049@uvahev.phys.Virginia.EDU>,
    				js9s@uvahev.phys.Virginia.EDU (jg_sun) writes:

> I have bounch of bdf files, and like to install them on a
> VMS machines. There are 3 questions:
>
> 1.  Does X server on OpenVMS recognize bdf and pcf files?

        VMS supplies the FONT command which  will compile BDF fonts into
    "SNF" fonts (Server Natural Fonts). Do: $HELP FONT for details.

>     I saw all those fnt files in sys$library, which are
>     supposed to be font file, but what are the ?

        The .FNT files in  SYS$LIBRARY  have  nothing  to  do with the X
    server  (DECwindows/Motif).   I  don't happen to know  what  product
    requires them (probably GKS), but you can ignore them.

> 2.  If the X server does use pcf files, in which directory
>     are they living? I can find anything like that anywhere.

        All the DECwindows  fonts  live in SYS$COMMON:[SYSFONT.DECW...],
    and use a file type of .DECW$FONT.

> 3. If the X server does not like bdf or pcf files, is there a
>    utility that can convert the files  to the format that the
>    X server can recognize ?

        It does, see above.  Since  the  DECwindows fonts live in system
    directories,  you'll need the help of your system manager to get the
    new fonts added.  I don't recall all  the  details,  but  I  believe
    there  is  a  command  file that needs to be run to update the "font
    directory" (which is NOT a disk directory, but a flat file) with the
    newly added fonts.

        There is probably  a  way  to  make  fonts  available  from your
    personal  account/directories.  I don't happen to know how.  Posting
    to  comp.os.vms  and/or  comp.windows.x.motif  might  generate  more
    responses.

    	-Ken
