Dear alpha/tiff people,

I received a lot of responses to my question:

"How do I get my hands on a tiff library for the Alpha
or XV for the alpha that handles tiff files.

The following people responded:

David Abbott 		<dfa@austin.unimelb.edu.au>
Ken Miller 		<ken@klab.caltech.edu>
Grant Denkinson 	<gwd10@geography.nottingham.ac.uk>
"Magnus Persson, Lunds datacentral" <Magnus.Persson@LDC.lu.se>
L.Rosenthaler" 		<rosenth@ppc.lpc.ethz.ch>
"Richard L. Dyson" 	<dyson@sunfish.Physics.UIowa.Edu>
 rsalz@osf.org

Thanks to you all. 

I took the tack suggested by David Abbott. Which essentially 
boiled down to a 4 step process described below, taken almost
verbatim from David Abbots suggestions:

1. 	ftp Sam Lefler's tifflib v3.3beta002 which has 64 bit
    	support.

	ftp ftp.sgi.com
	cd graphics/tiff
	binary
	get v3.3beta002.src.tar.Z

2. 	INSTALLATION:
	cp -p Makefile.sun Makefile
	edit tif_write.c and change at end of file
	TIFFSetWriteOffset(TIFF* tif, off_t off)
	to
	TIFFSetWriteOffset(TIFF* tif, toff_t off)

	then do the make.

3.	Then grab xv-3.0a from:
	ftp.cis.upenn.edu

	directory: pub/xv

4.	INSTALLATION
	untar etc..
	cd jpeg
	cp -p makefile.ansi makefile
	cd ..
	edit xv.h and add following...


	#ifdef __alpha
	# include <sys/syslimits.h> # for PATH_MAX (MAXPATHLEN)
	#endif
	... somewhere before MAXPATHLEN is used.

	Note that according to the xv 3.0 docs, the TIFF library does
	NOT work on 64 bit machines such as the Alpha.
	This is only true for TIFF
	libraries at v3.2 or earlier. v3.3 beta works, so replace the
	tiff directory with the contents of libtiff from v3.3
	 (or make a soft link).
	Then edit xvtiff.c and add near the start:


	#ifdef __alpha
	typedef unsigned int U_long;
	typedef int Long;
	#else
	#define U_long u_long
	typedef long Long;
	#endif
	
	
	and then change all u_long refrences to U_long and long to Long.

	If you like, you can edit the Makefile and add -Olimit 1300 to
	the CCOPTS line to get all functions to be optimised.
 
That should do it.
Another big thanks, especially to David Abbott.

Phil Neal

