>I have bounch of bdf files, and like to install them on a 
>VMS machines. There are 3 questions:
>
>1.  Does X server on OpenVMS recognize bdf and pcf files?  
>    I saw all those fnt files in sys$library, which are 
>    supposed to be font file, but what are the ?
>
  OpenVMS VAX supports it's own server natural format.

  OpenVMS AXP supports BDF, PCF, SNF (all the standard
  font formats supported by the MIT X11R5 sample server).


>2.  If the X server does use pcf files, in which directory 
>    are they living? I can find anything like that anywhere.
>
   A logical name DECW$FONT will give you the font path.  The
   path is a search list for the directories in SYS$COMMON:
   [SYSFONT.DECW...]

>
>3. If the X server does not like bdf or pcf files, is there a
>   utility that can convert the files  to the format that the 
>   X server can recognize ?
>
   The FONT command compiles BDF fonts into the natural format
   for the server.  The VAX generates DECW$FONT files, the AXP
   generates .PCF files.  The command is simply:

	$ FONT fontfile.bdf

   The directories in [SYSFONT.DECW...] labeled USER_* are
   there for your use, the other directories may get clobbered
   during a upgrade.

   Place all the font files, converted to the server natural
   format, into one of these areas, set your default to that
   subdirectory and type:

	$ FONT/DIR

   This will generate a font directory .DAT file that the server
   uses to find it's fonts.

   Reboot, or restart the server (from the SYSTEM account, type

	$ @DECW$STARTUP RESTART

   to pick up the new fonts.

   So, if you had font "foo.bdf" in your default directory, the
   actions would be (for an AXP):

	$ FONT foo.bdf
	$ COPY foo.pcf sys$common:[sysfont.decw.user_common]
        $ SET DEF sys$common:[sysfont.decw.user_common]
	$ FONT/DIR
	$ TYPE/PA DECW$FONT_DIRECTORY*.DAT
        $ @SYS$MANAGER:DECW$STARTUP RESTART

   The only change for the VAX would be the font name would be
   foo.decw$font instead of .pcf

   
