Our users can get their own disk drives and put them in their RAID 7 disk 
array platforms, thereby avoiding those exhorbitant margins that certain 
big RAID vendors with the older architectures get by insisting that users 
get "certified" drives directly from the RAID vendors.  In general, the 
users can select from a wide array of drives to run in their disk array 
platforms.

By the way, people keep asking us about those DEC drives made in the 
Bavaria plant of DEC's that just closed.  These are 4 GB drives and the 
shortage seems to be causing a big problem, from what we are hearing.

Regarding the VAX, a customer run benchmark test just came in with the 
following figures, which shows why DEC has been having problems with its 
storage business.

VAX 6630:

Applications:             Before RAID 7               With RAID 7      

DB                            7   hours               47 minutes
Payroll                       2   hours               15 minutes
Financials, internal          1.5 hours                4 minutes
Financials, external          3   hours               15 minutes

This is why many of us here think we might be able to turn around the DEC 
storage business with new technology.  BTW, regarding that posting asking 
about how a smaller company can buy out a larger company, the principle is 
quite clear:  leveraged buyouts.  You can read about them all the time on 
the financial pages.  The acquiring company borrows money to make the 
purchase, fixes the troubled company, often selling off underutilized 
assets and paying off the debt with the proceeds from the asset sales.

