In article <MIKEW.94May6164357@pooh.dosli.govt.nz> mikew@gopher.dosli.govt.nz (Mike Williams) writes:
>  The problem is that if the client crashes for any reason (which it does,
>  reasonably often), the TCP connection is not destroyed, and the server
>  stays around with it's stdout stream blocked.  Funnily enough, when the
>  PC is rebooted, the connection springs into life again, the conversation
>  completes, and the server retires peacefully.
>
>  * I'm surprised that the connection isn't closed when the client
>    application dies.  Is this a bug in the TCP/IP implementation?  (on
>    either the PC or Ultrix end)

When you say "the client crashes", do you mean the just application dies,
or the whole PC crashes?

If the PC crashes, there's no way for the server to know this.  It will
continue to try sending the reply, waiting for the client to acknowledge
it.  There should be a timeout, so eventually it should realize that the
client is never going to acknowledge.  But maybe you're rebooting the PC
before this timeout occurs (some systems may have very long timeouts).

If the PC stays up but the application dies, it sounds like a problem with
the WSOCKETS library not closing the connection when the application dies.

When you reboot the PC, the next retransmission by the server causes it to
send a RST (reset) to indicate that the connection no longer exists, which
causes the server to realize that the client died.

>  * Is there any way my server can detect when the client dies?

If the PC TCP/IP implementation has this limitation, probably not without
an application-level solution.

>  * Can anyone suggest a better protocol which would avoid these problems?

Require the client to reply to the server's response.  The server could
then have its own application-level timeout.
-- 
