There is  a switch (-class) for Xws and friends to set the visual class for 
the root window. When creating a window one usualy inherit the visual from it's
parent, hence the root window. However, when setting the root window to 
"PseudoColor" doesn't mean that one cannot create windows of other visual types.
A typical application should look at what visuals the X-server is advertising
and choose the one which for him, or die gracefully. He should NOT depend on the
default behaviour of the X-server...

In your case you should configure your X-server to start with PseudoColor for
the root window and your "True Color" apps should create True Color visuals
for them selves. Running the X-server with TrueColor root visual makes it 
unnecessary slow.

/Peter
