The U.S. Chapter of DECUS, the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society, 
this week announced the results of the Chapter's Board of Directors 
elections. Chapter members elected three new Board members from a field of 
seven candidates. Those participating in the vote also did not pass a 
referendum to change the Chapter's bylaws.

Those elected to the Board include: Geriann Goeransson of Fermi National 
Accelerator Laboratory, Batavia, IL; William Mayhew of Village Systems 
Workshop, Inc., Natick, MA; and James Sims of Grumman Data Systems, North 
Charleston, SC. Under the Chapter's current bylaws the three elected 
candidates will each serve two-year terms commencing on July 1, 1994.

A referendum change to the Chapter's bylaws received 65 percent of the 
vote, but did not receive the necessary support of two-thirds of those 
voting. The referendum, supported by the current Board of Directors, related 
to the composition of the Board of Directors, the election process, and the 
methods of nomination. 

"The intent of the referendum was to reorganize the Board of Directors, to  
allow members to vote directly for the Chapter president, to institute term 
limits for directors, and to have the membership select part of the 
elections committee. The outcome of the referendum does not affect other 
facets of our ongoing transformation process or how our activities are 
produced. We will continue with other aspects of our transformation which 
aggressively work to keep the Chapter in touch with current industry issues 
and directions, and contribute to the success or our members," noted Chapter 
President Margaret Knox.

The U.S. Chapter of DECUS, the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society, is 
an Association of Information Technology professionals interested in the 
products, services, and technologies of Digital Equipment Corporation and 
related vendors. The Association's purpose is to promote the unimpeded 
exchange of information, with the goal of helping its members and their 
organizations to be more successful. The Chapter provides members with means 
to enhance their professional development, forums for technical training, 
mechanisms for obtaining up-to-date information, advocacy programs, and 
opportunities for informal disclosure and interaction with professional 
colleagues of like interests.

